<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bozhou Chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">876 CE – 1600 CE (approx.) · capital Hailongtun</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The hereditary domain of the Yang family in northern Guizhou, which ruled for seven centuries as a native chieftaincy under Chinese dynasties until its destruction in 1600.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to family tradition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-duan">Yang Duan</a>, a man from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, recovered Bozhou, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zunyi">Zunyi</a>, from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanzhao">Nanzhao</a> forces in 876 and was granted hereditary authority over it. His descendants ruled the mountainous region as local lords, accepting nominal titles from successive Chinese courts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Yang lords governed a mixed population of Han settlers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miao-people">Miao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelao-people">Gelao</a> and other peoples as tusi, native chieftains who paid tribute and supplied troops while running their own affairs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Yuan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> dynasties confirmed their titles. During the Mongol wars the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-song">Southern Song</a> built the mountain fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hailongtun">Hailongtun</a> in 1257, later the seat of the Yang family.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-yinglong">Yang Yinglong</a> rebelled against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> in 1589. In 1600 a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hualong">Li Hualong</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hailongtun">Hailongtun</a> and Yang Yinglong killed himself. The chieftaincy was abolished and replaced by regular prefectures under direct imperial rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 876 CE – 1600 CE (approx.)">